      50209 accounted for             dbo.uspCloseApprovedCaseFiles checking its own work: it fires when the sweep closed N case files but wrote a number of audit rows that is not N, and it rolls the batch back. Reaching it means breaking the UPDATE's OUTPUT clause first, which is the statement being asserted -- a test that edited it would be measuring the edit. It is here because a set-based writer that silently under-logs is the exact failure logs.uspRecordDataChange's notes warn about, and an assertion is cheaper than a code review.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 
