14.  Coverage of Appendix B, measured rather than asserted
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Throwable from sys.sql_modules in the house idiom ....... 131
     Ever recorded in logs.ExecutionLog ..................... 124
     Raised on purpose by this file ......................... 103
     Throwable but unobservable, with a reason .............. 8
     Throwable, unobserved and unaccounted for .............. none
     In the ledger but outside the harvest .................. 50099
 
     Numbers raised by SCRIPTS rather than modules are outside the harvest by construction, because a script
     leaves nothing in sys.sql_modules: E-50080, E-50085, E-50086 and E-50087 in 900_bootstrap_first_admin.sql,
     and E-50210 and E-50211 in 135_audit_triggers.sql. No file under _tests raises any of the six, and none of
     them is a refusal a CALLER can meet -- the four bootstrap numbers are what a second bootstrap or an
     out-of-order install is told, and the two audit numbers are 135 asserting its own triggers at install time.
     A database this file can run against at all is one where 135 did not throw, and a database with profiles in
     it is one where a second bootstrap would raise E-50080. They are proved by the install, not by a test.
 
     Three more numbers are in the ledger without being raised here, and each has one place that does raise it:
     E-50220, E-50221 and E-50222 are the password-change refusals, and _tests/040_identity_and_authn.sql
     section 13 provokes all three on an account whose whole fixture exists for them. E-50224 is 110's own
     closing report, which probes it on every deployment. E-50230 is probed BOTH here and by 150's closing
     report, and the duplication is deliberate: the report proves the number exists, and section 2j proves it is
     raised before the session is resolved. A number observed on every install is not a number this file has to
     raise -- but it is a number somebody has to, and the sentence above says who.
 
PASS  080_error_catalogue: every number a shipped module can throw is either in the ledger or accounted for.
